## Key Features

### Asynchronous Job Processing

Long-running tasks are submitted to a background queue instead of being executed directly during an API request.

This separates job submission from job execution and allows the API to respond without waiting for the task to finish.

### Priority-Based Processing

Jobs can be assigned different priority levels:

```text
HIGH
MEDIUM
LOW
```

Higher-priority jobs are processed before lower-priority jobs when multiple eligible jobs are waiting in the queue.

### Delayed Job Execution

Jobs can be scheduled to execute after a specified delay.

Example:

```text
Job Submitted
      |
      v
Delay: 10 seconds
      |
      v
Job Becomes Eligible
      |
      v
Worker Processes Job
```

### Scheduled Jobs

The platform supports scheduled background tasks that can execute at predefined intervals.

Examples include:

* Periodic report generation
* Scheduled data processing
* Recurring maintenance tasks
* Automated background operations

### Automatic Retries

Failed jobs can automatically retry a configurable number of times.

The platform uses exponential backoff to increase the delay between retry attempts.

```text
Attempt 1
   |
   X Failed
   |
   v
Wait 2 seconds
   |
   v
Attempt 2
   |
   X Failed
   |
   v
Wait 4 seconds
   |
   v
Attempt 3
```

This reduces repeated immediate failures and provides temporary failures with time to recover.

### Dead Letter Queue

Jobs that continue to fail after exhausting their retry attempts are moved to a Dead Letter Queue.

```text
Main Queue
    |
    v
Worker
    |
    +---- Success ---> Completed
    |
    +---- Failure
           |
           v
         Retry
           |
           +---- Success ---> Completed
           |
           +---- Final Failure
                    |
                    v
                  DLQ
```

The DLQ preserves failed job information for investigation and potential manual reprocessing.

### Multiple Workers

Multiple worker processes can connect to the same BullMQ queue.

```text
                 Redis
                   |
          +--------+--------+
          |        |        |
          v        v        v
       Worker 1 Worker 2 Worker 3
          |        |        |
          v        v        v
        Job A    Job B    Job C
```

This allows available workers to process different jobs concurrently and improves overall throughput.

### Configurable Concurrency

Workers can process multiple jobs concurrently based on the configured concurrency level.

For example:

```text
Worker 1
  ├── Job A
  ├── Job B
  └── Job C
```

Concurrency can be tuned according to the workload and available system resources.

### Job Progress Tracking

Long-running jobs report their execution progress from 0% to 100%.

```text
0% → 20% → 40% → 60% → 80% → 100%
```

Progress information is stored in MongoDB and also propagated through BullMQ events.

### Persistent Job State

MongoDB maintains the application's persistent representation of each job.

Supported states include:

```text
QUEUED
ACTIVE
COMPLETED
FAILED
DEAD
```

This allows job information to remain available even after the worker finishes processing.

### Batch Job Submission

Multiple jobs can be submitted through a single API request.

Example:

```json
{
  "jobs": [
    {
      "type": "GENERATE_REPORT",
      "priority": "HIGH"
    },
    {
      "type": "SEND_EMAIL",
      "priority": "MEDIUM"
    },
    {
      "type": "PROCESS_IMAGE",
      "priority": "LOW"
    }
  ]
}
```

### Real-Time Monitoring

The React dashboard receives job updates through Socket.IO.

Instead of repeatedly requesting the backend:

```text
React
  |
  | GET /api/jobs
  | GET /api/jobs
  | GET /api/jobs
  v
Backend
```

the platform uses real-time events:

```text
Worker
   |
   v
BullMQ / Redis
   |
   v
Socket.IO
   |
   v
React Dashboard
```

This allows job status and progress to update without manually refreshing the page.

